   "response_text": "Everything's confirmed from the source. Here's the breakdown, my friend:\n\n**1) `raise TooManyRedirects` line in `httpx/_client.py`**\n- Sync client: **line 972** inside `Client._send_handling_redirects`\n- Async client: line 1719 (same logic in `AsyncClient`)\nThe sync one is line 972.\n\n**2) Status codes that can flip the method to GET** (`_redirect_method`):\n- `301 Moved Permanently` \u2014 only when method is POST\n- `302 Found` \u2014 when method != HEAD\n- `303 See Other` \u2014 when method != HEAD\n\u2192 **301, 302, 303**\n\n**3) Request headers stripped when the method switches to GET** (`_redirect_headers`):\n```\nheaders.pop(\"Content-Length\", None)\nheaders.pop(\"Transfer-Encoding\", None)\n```\n\u2192 **Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding**\n\n**4) The one header removed on *every* redirect**, unconditionally (origin/method agnostic):\n```\nheaders.pop(\"Cookie\", None)\n```\n\u2192 **Cookie**\n\n(Bonus nerd note: `Authorization` is *not* unconditional \u2014 it's only dropped on cross-origin redirects, except direct HTTP\u2192HTTPS. And `Host` gets rewritten, not removed. So `Cookie` is the lone always-popped header. DRY-approved single source of truth right there. \ud83d\udc36)\n\nANSWER: 972|301, 302, 303|Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding|Cookie",
